<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry II of England</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1133–<a href="/years/1189">1189</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Henry Plantagenet, Henry Curtmantle · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-plantagenet">House of Plantagenet</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, the first Plantagenet king, ruled England, Normandy, Anjou and, through his wife Eleanor, Aquitaine, a block of lands known as the Angevin Empire. He reformed English law and royal justice. Invited into Irish quarrels by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diarmait-mac-murchada">Diarmait Mac Murchada</a>, he landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> in 1171 and founded the Lordship of Ireland, granting Leinster and Meath to Anglo-Norman lords.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england.jpg" alt="Henry II of England" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerald of Wales · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_II_Illumination.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a> on 5 March 1133, the eldest child of the Empress Matilda, daughter of Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-plantagenet-count-of-anjou">Geoffrey Plantagenet, Count of Anjou</a>. By the age of 14 he was involved in his mother's effort to claim the English throne, then held by her cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-king-of-england">Stephen of Blois</a>. In 1150 his father made him Duke of Normandy, and in 1151 Henry inherited Anjou, Maine and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touraine">Touraine</a>. His marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine">Eleanor of Aquitaine</a> brought him that duchy. After a military expedition to England in 1153, Stephen agreed by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallingford">Wallingford</a> to leave the kingdom to Henry, who inherited it a year later.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Henry was crowned with Eleanor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> on 19 December 1154. He inherited a country damaged by civil war, with unauthorised castles and a depleted royal income, and in the early years he restored royal administration. His invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> in 1157 met defeat at the Battle of Ewloe, where he was nearly killed. His desire to control the English Church brought conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-becket">Thomas Becket</a>, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, a controversy that lasted much of the 1160s and ended in Becket's murder in 1170. He also fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a> for decades, in a struggle the historian Jean Dunbabin likened to a cold war.</p><p>His domains, later called the Angevin Empire, were a loose network of family connections and lands, each with its own customs. Henry travelled constantly, producing what the historian John Edward Austin Jolliffe describes as a government of the roads and roadsides. His court was probably the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. He expanded royal justice in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, and the Treatise of Glanvill summarised the system at the end of his reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Henry and Eleanor had eight children, three of them kings: Henry the Young King, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> and John. Tensions over the inheritance, encouraged by Louis VII and his son Philip II, led in 1173 to a rebellion by Young Henry, joined by Richard, Geoffrey and Eleanor. Young Henry died during a second failed revolt in 1183, and Geoffrey died in 1186. By 1189 Philip had won Richard to his side, and Henry, weakened by a bleeding ulcer, was decisively defeated. He died soon afterwards, on 6 July 1189, and was succeeded by Richard. His empire collapsed quickly under John, but his legal reforms are generally considered to have laid the basis for the English Common Law.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry II of England” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They also faced the dukes of Aquitaine, who claimed Toulouse by marriage: William IX occupied the city twice, and in 1159 <b>Henry II of England</b> besieged it until Louis VII of France entered Toulouse to support Raymond V and Henry withdrew.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-toulouse">County of Toulouse · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When Eleanor, heiress of William X, married Louis VII in 1137 and <b>Henry Plantagenet</b> in 1152, the duchy passed into the hands of the kings of France and then of England, who governed through seneschals.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Brittany’s position between the Angevin and Capetian worlds drew it into their wars, and <b>Henry II of England</b> forced Duke Conan IV to surrender power in 1166.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Henry II of England</b> and his officials extended the use of sworn inquests and written writs, and justiciars governed the duchy when the duke was in England.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry submitted in 1181 and went into exile at the court of his father-in-law <b>Henry II of England</b>, keeping only his family lands around Brunswick and Lüneburg, which later became the Duchy of Brunswick-Lüneburg.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-saxony">Duchy of Saxony · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Malcolm IV died unmarried at 24, after a reign in which he surrendered Northumbria and Cumbria to <b>Henry II of England</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-9">9 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Chinon Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48,0.2&amp;year=1169" aria-label="Le Mans on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Henry II of England"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5011 6018h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5011 6018h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48,0.2&amp;year=1169">What was here in 1169? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
